# PATENT CLAIM

**Claim 1.** A video encoder for encoding a sequence of pictures, the encoder comprising:

a processor and a memory storing instructions that, when executed by the processor, configure the encoder to perform operations comprising:

**(a) Motion Vector Prediction Module**, configured to:

(i) partition a current picture into a plurality of coding units, each coding unit comprising one or more prediction units;

(ii) for a current prediction unit, identify a set of spatial candidate motion vectors from neighboring prediction units located at predetermined positions relative to the current prediction unit, the predetermined positions comprising a left-bottom position, a left position, an above-right position, an above position, and an above-left position within a same picture as the current prediction unit;

(iii) identify a set of temporal candidate motion vectors from one or more co-located prediction units in one or more reference pictures temporally distinct from the current picture, wherein each temporal candidate motion vector is scaled based on a ratio of a first temporal distance between the current picture and a reference picture referenced by the current prediction unit to a second temporal distance between the co-located picture and a reference picture referenced by the co-located prediction unit;

(iv) construct a motion vector predictor candidate list by combining the spatial candidate motion vectors and the temporal candidate motion vectors, and removing redundant candidates from the candidate list based on a comparison of motion vector values and associated reference picture indices;

(v) select a motion vector predictor from the motion vector predictor candidate list based on a rate-distortion optimization criterion, and encode an index identifying the selected motion vector predictor within the motion vector predictor candidate list into a bitstream;

(vi) compute a motion vector difference between an actual motion vector determined for the current prediction unit and the selected motion vector predictor, and encode the motion vector difference into the bitstream;

**(b) Residual Coding Module**, configured to:

(i) generate a residual block by subtracting a prediction block, generated using the actual motion vector, from a corresponding original block of the current picture;

(ii) transform the residual block into a plurality of transform coefficients using a block transform;

(iii) determine a predicted quantization parameter for a current transform block by:

(A) identifying available quantization parameters associated with a left-neighboring transform block, an above-neighboring transform block, and a previously coded transform block within the current picture, in a predetermined order of availability;

(B) computing an average of the available quantization parameters among the left-neighboring transform block and the above-neighboring transform block when both are available, and using the previously coded transform block's quantization parameter as the predicted quantization parameter when neither the left-neighboring nor above-neighboring quantization parameters are available;

(iv) computing a quantization parameter delta representing a difference between an actual quantization parameter selected for the current transform block and the predicted quantization parameter;

(v) quantizing the plurality of transform coefficients using a quantization step size derived from the actual quantization parameter, to produce a plurality of quantized transform coefficients; and

(vi) encoding the quantization parameter delta and the plurality of quantized transform coefficients into the bitstream;

wherein the motion vector prediction module and the residual coding module operate cooperatively to generate a compressed bitstream representative of the sequence of pictures with reduced bit-rate overhead for motion vector signaling and quantization parameter signaling.

---

**Claim 2.** The encoder of claim 1, wherein constructing the motion vector predictor candidate list further comprises limiting a total number of candidates in the candidate list to a predetermined maximum number, and wherein the encoder is further configured to insert a zero motion vector candidate into the candidate list when a number of available candidates is fewer than the predetermined maximum number.

**Claim 3.** The encoder of claim 1, wherein identifying the set of spatial candidate motion vectors comprises evaluating the predetermined positions in a defined priority sequence and terminating the evaluation upon identifying a first available candidate satisfying a predetermined reference picture matching criterion.

**Claim 4.** The encoder of claim 1, wherein the block transform comprises a plurality of selectable transform sizes, and the residual coding module is further configured to select a transform size for the residual block based on a quad-tree partitioning of the coding unit and to signal the selected transform size in the bitstream.

**Claim 5.** The encoder of claim 1, wherein the previously coded transform block used to determine the predicted quantization parameter comprises a most-recently-coded transform block in decoding order preceding the current transform block, and wherein the predicted quantization parameter for a first transform block in a slice is set equal to a slice-level initial quantization parameter signaled in a slice header.

**Claim 6.** The encoder of claim 1, wherein the quantization parameter delta is encoded using a variable-length binarization scheme in which smaller magnitude deltas are assigned shorter codewords than larger magnitude deltas.

**Claim 7.** The encoder of claim 1, wherein the temporal candidate motion vectors are derived from a co-located prediction unit selected from either a first reference picture list or a second reference picture list based on a flag signaled at a slice level.

**Claim 8.** A method for encoding video data, the method comprising the operations recited in claim 1, performed by one or more processors.

**Claim 9.** A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, cause the one or more processors to perform the operations recited in claim 1.

# PATENT CLAIM

**Claim 1.** A video codec device comprising:

a processor; and

a memory coupled to the processor and storing instructions that, when executed by the processor, cause the video codec device to:

receive a video signal comprising a plurality of frames, each frame partitioned into a plurality of coding blocks;

derive a motion vector predictor for a current coding block among the plurality of coding blocks by:

identifying a set of spatially neighboring blocks and at least one temporally co-located block associated with the current coding block;

extracting a plurality of candidate motion vectors from the set of spatially neighboring blocks and the at least one temporally co-located block;

constructing a motion vector predictor candidate list from the plurality of candidate motion vectors according to a predetermined priority order;

selecting, based on an index signaled in the video signal, a motion vector predictor from the motion vector predictor candidate list;

determine a motion vector for the current coding block based on the selected motion vector predictor and a motion vector difference obtained from the video signal;

generate a prediction block for the current coding block using the determined motion vector;

derive a residual block by combining the current coding block with the prediction block;

transform the residual block to generate a block of transform coefficients;

quantize the block of transform coefficients to produce a block of quantized coefficients arranged in a two-dimensional array;

partition the block of quantized coefficients into a plurality of subsets, each subset comprising a predetermined number of the quantized coefficients arranged in a sub-array smaller than the two-dimensional array;

scan the quantized coefficients within each subset of the plurality of subsets according to a scan pattern selected from a plurality of predefined scan patterns, wherein the same scan pattern is applied consistently to each and every subset of the plurality of subsets irrespective of a position of the each and every subset within the two-dimensional array;

scan the plurality of subsets themselves according to a subset scan order corresponding to the scan pattern applied within each subset, such that the subset scan order and the intra-subset scan pattern are selected from a common scan pattern type comprising at least one of a diagonal scan, a horizontal scan, or a vertical scan;

generate a serialized sequence of the quantized coefficients based on the scanning of the quantized coefficients within each subset and the scanning of the plurality of subsets; and

entropy encode the serialized sequence of the quantized coefficients to produce a bitstream representing the current coding block.

---

**Claim 2.** The video codec device of claim 1, wherein the scan pattern is selected based on an intra-prediction mode associated with the current coding block when the current coding block is intra-predicted.

**Claim 3.** The video codec device of claim 1, wherein the plurality of predefined scan patterns comprises a diagonal up-right scan pattern, a horizontal scan pattern, and a vertical scan pattern, and wherein the instructions further cause the video codec device to signal, in the bitstream, a scan pattern index identifying the selected scan pattern.

**Claim 4.** The video codec device of claim 1, wherein the predetermined number of the quantized coefficients comprised in each subset is four arranged in a two-by-two sub-array.

**Claim 5.** The video codec device of claim 1, wherein the instructions further cause the video codec device to signal, for each subset of the plurality of subsets, a subset significance flag indicating whether the subset contains at least one non-zero quantized coefficient, prior to entropy encoding the serialized sequence.

**Claim 6.** The video codec device of claim 1, wherein constructing the motion vector predictor candidate list comprises:

determining availability of the set of spatially neighboring blocks based on at least one of a coding mode or a boundary condition of the current coding block;

responsive to determining that a threshold number of available candidate motion vectors is not satisfied, deriving a scaled candidate motion vector based on a temporal distance between a reference frame associated with the at least one temporally co-located block and a reference frame associated with the current coding block; and

inserting the scaled candidate motion vector into the motion vector predictor candidate list.

**Claim 7.** The video codec device of claim 1, wherein the instructions further cause the video codec device to:

decode the bitstream to reconstruct the block of quantized coefficients by inverse scanning the serialized sequence of the quantized coefficients according to the same scan pattern applied consistently to each and every subset;

inverse quantize and inverse transform the reconstructed block of quantized coefficients to generate a reconstructed residual block; and

combine the reconstructed residual block with a corresponding prediction block generated using a decoded motion vector to reconstruct the current coding block.

**Claim 8.** The video codec device of claim 1, wherein the two-dimensional array corresponds to a transform unit having a size selected from a group consisting of 4×4, 8×8, 16×16, and 32×32.

**Claim 9.** The video codec device of claim 1, wherein the subset scan order proceeds from a subset containing a lowest-frequency quantized coefficient to a subset containing a highest-frequency quantized coefficient, and wherein, within each subset, the scan pattern proceeds from a lowest-frequency position to a highest-frequency position of the sub-array.

**Claim 10.** A method performed by the video codec device of claim 1, the method comprising the steps recited in claim 1.

# Independent Patent Claim

**Claim 1.** A video encoding system comprising:

a memory configured to store video data comprising a plurality of coding units, each coding unit having spatial neighbors including a left neighboring coding unit, an upper neighboring coding unit, and a previous coding unit in decoding order; and

a processor coupled to the memory and configured to:

determine, for a current coding unit, availability status of a left neighboring quantization parameter associated with the left neighboring coding unit, an upper neighboring quantization parameter associated with the upper neighboring coding unit, and a previous quantization parameter associated with the previous coding unit, wherein a neighboring quantization parameter is deemed available when the corresponding neighboring coding unit exists, has been previously encoded, and resides within a same slice and same tile as the current coding unit;

select, from among the left neighboring quantization parameter, the upper neighboring quantization parameter, and the previous quantization parameter, exactly two valid quantization parameters according to a predetermined priority order, wherein the predetermined priority order preferentially selects the left neighboring quantization parameter and the upper neighboring quantization parameter when both are available, and substitutes the previous quantization parameter for whichever of the left neighboring quantization parameter or the upper neighboring quantization parameter is unavailable;

compute a predicted quantization parameter for the current coding unit by averaging the two selected valid quantization parameters;

determine a quantization parameter delta representing a difference between an actual quantization parameter to be applied to the current coding unit and the predicted quantization parameter;

quantize residual data of the current coding unit using the actual quantization parameter;

generate a motion vector predictor for the current coding unit based on motion vectors of one or more spatially or temporally neighboring coding units, and derive a motion vector difference representing a difference between an actual motion vector of the current coding unit and the motion vector predictor; and

entropy encode the quantization parameter delta, the motion vector difference, and the quantized residual data into a bitstream.
